Our publishing tool renders chapter numbers and copyright years as roman numerals, and QA says some years come out malformed. Set up /workspace with vitest and save src/roman.ts:

const PAIRS: Array<[number, string]> = [
  [1000, "M"], [900, "CM"], [500, "D"], [400, "CD"],
  [100, "C"], [50, "L"], [10, "X"], [9, "IX"],
  [5, "V"], [4, "IV"], [1, "I"],
];

export function toRoman(n: number): string {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1 || n > 3999) throw new RangeError(String(n));
  let out = "";
  let rest = n;
  for (const [value, sym] of PAIRS) {
    while (rest >= value) {
      out += sym;
      rest -= value;
    }
  }
  return out;
}

The table is missing the [90, "XC"] and [40, "XL"] entries, so 40 renders as XXXX and 94 as LXXXXIV. Write src/roman.test.ts using test.each with at least 25 rows: 1, 4, 9, 14, 40, 44, 49, 90, 94, 99, 400, 900, 1990, 2026, 3999 among them, each with the CORRECT expected numeral (derive them by hand, comment the additive breakdown for 1990 = MCMXC). Run npx vitest run, watch the 40/90 family fail, fix the table, and rerun green. Then add a total-function property loop (plain code, no library): for every n in 1 to 3999, toRoman(n) matches ^M{0,3}(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$ and no two n produce the same string (use a Set). Both suites green.

